</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Exact-match metric of different Llama models on the evaluation set of GSM8k under different KV cache quantization regimes. For each setup, the average size of the KV cache is shown in parentheses.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the performance of InnerQ with other setups by applying them to pre-trained models and running few-shot evaluation on the evaluation set of the GSM8k dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.
As reported in Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2 Evaluation Performance and Cache Size ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ InnerQ: Hardware-aware Tuning-free Quantization of KV Cache for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, InnerQ maintains the metric performance of the baseline while significantly reducing the size of the KV cache.
InnerQ also shows a superior evaluation performance compared to both KIVI variants while having a slightly higher cache size due to mask used by hybrid quantization.
We study the trade-off of the hybrid quantization mask in terms of memory usage and metric performance in Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="6.1 Quantization Mode ‣ 6 Ablation Studies ‣ InnerQ: Hardware-aware Tuning-free Quantization of KV Cache for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
